      </div>
    <div class="max-w-2xl"><!-- resume narrow column -->

      <!-- Confidence -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">Confidence can come from repetition—not just reassurance</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">It's worth being precise about this one, because it's easy to overstate. Violin lessons don't automatically produce a confident student. Praise and encouragement matter, and a good teacher offers plenty of both. But there's a different kind of confidence that comes from somewhere else: from attempting something repeatedly, gradually understanding how it works, and eventually realizing you can do something that used to feel impossible.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">That kind of confidence is earned, not given. It doesn't need to be told to you—you already know, because you were there for every attempt that came before the one that finally worked. It also tends to hold up better under pressure, because it isn't dependent on someone else's approval to feel true.</p>

      <!-- Attention -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">You spend time making something instead of consuming something</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Playing violin asks for a specific, embodied kind of attention. You're listening for whether a note is in tune. You're coordinating a bow arm, a left hand, posture, and breath, usually all at once. You're feeling for the physical difference between a tense shoulder and a relaxed one, and noticing what that difference does to the sound.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">That's a different kind of engagement than the kind most of a day tends to involve. It doesn't require treating screens or technology as the enemy to notice that this—making a sound with your own hands, in real time, with your full attention—is a genuinely different experience than watching or scrolling through something someone else made. Both have their place. But a student who spends real time creating develops a familiarity with focused, active attention that's worth having, simply because it doesn't happen as often as it used to.</p>

      <!-- Mistakes -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">Mistakes become information</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">A wrong note is not a referendum on whether someone belongs in music. It's information: about intonation, about a finger placed slightly too high or too low, about a shift that needs to happen a little sooner.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Teaching that treats mistakes this way—as data to work with rather than evidence of failure—tends to produce students who stay curious under difficulty instead of shutting down. A missed shift gets investigated, not mourned. A scratchy bow stroke gets adjusted, not apologized for.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">That distinction matters, because the alternative—treating every mistake as a small failure—usually just teaches a student to avoid anything hard enough to risk one. Productive struggle and discouraging struggle can look similar from the outside. The difference is almost always in how the mistake gets handled once it happens—and <a href="/insights/how-much-frustration-is-normal-when-learning-violin" class="text-purplePrimary underline hover:text-purple-800 transition-colors duration-150">how much frustration is actually normal</a> on the way to getting something right is a question worth understanding on its own.</p>

      <!-- Mid-article CTA -->
      <div class="mt-10 flex">
        <a href="/about" class="btn-secondary" aria-label="Learn about Rae's teaching approach at Vibrations Violin Studio">
          Learn About Rae's Teaching Approach
        </a>
